
Jurassic Pterosaurs (mostly smaller, earlier forms; long-tailed or transitional)These tend to be more agile but generally slower in sustained/top speed than big Cretaceous soarers.Rhamphorhynchus — Classic long-tailed; good glider/marine hunter, solid medium speed.

*Pterodactylus — Iconic small-medium pterodactyloid; versatile.
Scaphognathus — Basal, similar to Rhamphorhynchus.
Campylognathoides — Early form with decent flight capabilities.
Darwinopterus — Transitional wukongopterid; good agility.
Kunpengopterus / Wukongopterus — Similar transitional forms.
Dimorphodon — Big head, shorter wings; more burst/maneuverable than fast.
Eudimorphodon — Early Triassic–Jurassic; basal traits.
*Anurognathus — Tiny insectivore; extremely agile but low top speed.
Jeholopterus — Anurognathid; similar small, fuzzy, maneuverable.
*Sordes — Small, fuzzy basal form; low cruise speed.
*Nemicolopterus — One of the tiniest; slowest overall.


